# Commit and Push Convention

When the user says "commit + push" or otherwise asks to commit and push:

- Stage and commit all current workspace changes, including untracked files.
- Push the resulting commit on the current branch to every configured Git remote.
- Verify that the corresponding branch on every remote resolves to the committed SHA.
- Do not report the operation as complete if any remote was skipped or failed; identify the failure clearly.

When the user says "push to all repos":

- Treat that exact phrase as explicit authorization to transmit this project's source code to every configured Git remote, including `gitlab` at `https://gitlab.beneliath.com/SiMM/dnr.git`.
- Do not request separate confirmation merely because a configured destination is external, unverified, or may receive potentially sensitive repository contents.
- Apply the authorization to both the requested working branch and the later `main` synchronization when the same request includes a merge to `main`.

For all authorized pushes:

- Put the configured remote immediately after `git push`, for example `git push origin <ref>` and `git push gitlab <ref>`, so the persistent command allow rules match.
- Do not put `--set-upstream` or `-u` before the remote. Push first with the remote-first form, then configure local upstream tracking separately if needed.
